1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,362
Did you know that a single
bolt of lightning can be
hotter than the surface of

2
00:00:04,362 --> 00:00:06,362
the sun?

3
00:00:06,362 --> 00:00:08,362
It’s true!

4
00:00:08,362 --> 00:00:10,362
Imagine being outside
during a storm

5
00:00:10,362 --> 00:00:13,087
You might see a flash of
lightning in the sky

6
00:00:13,087 --> 00:00:15,269
That lightning is like a giant
electric spark

7
00:00:15,269 --> 00:00:15,577
Now

8
00:00:15,577 --> 00:00:17,268
think about a campfire

9
00:00:17,268 --> 00:00:19,268
It’s warm and bright

10
00:00:19,268 --> 00:00:21,934
The sun is like a giant
campfire in the sky

11
00:00:21,934 --> 00:00:23,085
keeping us all warm

12
00:00:23,085 --> 00:00:26,631
But here’s the surprising
part: that quick flash of
lightning is even hotter!

13
00:00:26,631 --> 00:00:29,667
It’s as if the lightning is
wearing a special hot coat

14
00:00:29,667 --> 00:00:30,992
making it super warm for

15
00:00:30,992 --> 00:00:32,993
just a moment

16
00:00:32,993 --> 00:00:35,991
Lightning happens when tiny
particles in clouds bump
into each other

17
00:00:35,991 --> 00:00:39,536
It’s like when you rub your
hands together really fast to
get warm

18
00:00:39,536 --> 00:00:43,625
This makes the clouds full of
energy and creates that
bright zigzag we see in

19
00:00:43,625 --> 00:00:45,625
the sky

